<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n6001" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">caecĭtas</span></b> , ātis,
			f.
		caecus, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>blindness.</i>
		</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<b>Lit.</b> (rare but in good prose), <i><span class="en">Cic.
				Tusc. 5, 39, 113</span></i>; <i><span class="en">5, 38, 111</span></i>; <i><span class="en">id. Fin.
				5, 28, 84</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Plin. 11, 37, 55, § 149</span></i>;
				<i><span class="en">12, 8, 18, §
				34</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b>Trop.</b>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">furorem autem esse rati sunt mentis ad omnia
					caecitatem,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Cic.
					Tusc. 3, 5, 11</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">in furore animi et caecitate,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id.
					Dom. 50, 129</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">mentis,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id. ib. 40, 105</span></i>
			; cf.: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">an tibi luminis obesset caecitas plus quam
					libidinis,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id.
					Har. Resp. 18, 38</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
